---
// The founding story — the most personal page on the site.
//
// COPY IS ROB'S, VERBATIM. First person, his family, his mother. Do not
// paraphrase it, do not tighten it, do not "improve" the rhythm. If it needs to
// change, it changes because he changed it.
//
// PHOTOS ARE NOT IN THE REPO YET. public/images/family/ does not exist. Every
// <figure> below carries a placeholder frame in its place, sized to hold the
// picture named in its label. The captions are already final — only the images
// and their alt text are outstanding, and alt text has to be written by someone
// who can actually see them.
//
// Deliberately NOT filled with the Homer template's stock gallery images that
// happen to sit in public/assets/images/. Dressing stock photography as
// somebody's dead mother is not a placeholder, it is a lie.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The real Aunt Mabel — our founding story"
  description="Aunt Mabel was a real person. The true family story behind the daily calls — Lake Charles, Treasure Island 1942, Hurricane Laura, and a promise to show up."
  ogTitle="The real Aunt Mabel"
  ogDescription="She was my great-aunt. She remembered everyone's name. Her photo is our logo, and her spirit is the whole idea."
>
  <div class="wrap story">
    <header class="story-head">
      <p class="kicker">Our story</p>
      <h1>The real Aunt Mabel</h1>
    </header>

    <p class="opening">
      That photo you see everywhere on this site — the woman with the warm smile and
      the butterfly brooch? That's not a stock photo, and it's not a design choice.
      That's my Aunt Mabel.
    </p>

    <figure class="plate portrait">
      <div class="photo-slot tall" data-slot="Aunt Mabel — sepia portrait, the one used as the logo">
        <span>Photograph</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption>Aunt Mabel — the real one. Her smile is our logo.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      She was my great-aunt — my grandfather's sister. I only saw her a handful of
      times a year, at holidays and at the family reunion. But she left a tremendous
      mark on me, the way certain people do. She remembered everyone's name. Every
      child, every cousin, every new baby in a family that never stopped growing —
      Aunt Mabel knew your name, asked about your life, and meant it. She and all of
      my grandfather's siblings were like that: an infectious, kind smile. Laughter.
      Kindness. Never drama. Always there for you.
    </p>

    <figure class="plate">
      <div class="photo-pair">
        <div class="photo-slot wide" data-slot="1950s living room, full of children">
          <span>Photograph</span>
        </div>
        <div class="photo-slot wide" data-slot="Generations portrait">
          <span>Photograph</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The family reunions. Aunt Mabel knew every name in every one of these rooms.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      My grandfather, Robert L. Miller, died before I was born, so I never knew him —
      but I know who he was. An architect, graduated from Tulane. A Navy man who
      served in World War II. A Mason, and a contributing member of Lake Charles,
      Louisiana — my hometown, a city he spent his life helping build. He and my
      grandmother married at nineteen on Treasure Island in 1942, at the Navy base
      near San Francisco, just before he shipped off to war. That's them in the
      photograph — him in his dress blues, her in lace.
    </p>

    <figure class="plate">
      <div class="photo-slot tall" data-slot="Treasure Island wedding, 1942 — dress blues and lace">
        <span>Photograph</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Married at nineteen on Treasure Island, 1942, just before he shipped off to war.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      When he came home, he went to college, raised five children — my mother Kathy
      among them — and all the while, he and my grandmother were caring at home for a
      child who needed constant care. They didn't talk about it as a burden. They just
      showed up, every day, for the people they loved. That's what that whole family did.
    </p>

    <figure class="plate">
      <div class="photo-slot wide" data-slot="1993 — the women of the family gathered">
        <span>Photograph</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption>Decades later — still gathered, still laughing.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    {/* The hinge of the whole page. Given its own air deliberately: no photo, no
        ornament, nothing to look at but the sentences. */}
    <section class="quiet" aria-label="Rob's mother">
      <p class="line">My mother died when I was thirteen.</p>

      <p>
        I want to be honest about what that means here, because it's the truest part
        of this story. I never got to do the thing this service exists to do. I never
        got to be the grown son who calls to check on his mother. Never got to worry
        about whether she'd eaten, whether she'd taken her medicine, whether she was
        lonely in a quiet house. Never got to care for her the way she cared for me.
        That chance was simply gone before I was old enough to take it.
      </p>

      <p class="line-em">
        Most people build something like Aunt Mabel because they're worried about
        their mom. I built it because I never got the chance to be.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p>
      Years later, Hurricane Laura tore through Lake Charles — my hometown, my
      grandfather's town, the place his buildings stand. It's also where my family
      rests. My mother, Aunt Mabel, my grandparents — they're all buried there, in the
      same ground the storm crossed. I've spent years doing disaster response with the
      Cajun Navy Ground Force, and I can tell you what we found there and what we find
      after every storm: elderly people, alone. Unreached. Days going by before anyone
      thought to check. Somebody's mother, in a dark house with no power and no phone
      call coming. Every one of them was someone's Aunt Mabel. Every one of them
      deserved what my family gave so freely at every reunion — someone who knew their
      name and showed up.
    </p>

    <p>
      So when we built a service whose whole job is to show up every day — to call, to
      remember your name, to ask how you slept and whether you've eaten, to genuinely
      care how you're doing, and to reach your family the moment something's wrong —
      there was only one name it could carry.
    </p>

    <p>
      Aunt Mabel is what her spirit sounds like: warm, unhurried, glad to hear your
      voice. The kind of call that leaves you a little lighter than it found you.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you still have your mother — your father, your grandmother, your Aunt Mabel —
      you have something I'd give anything for: the chance to care for them. Let us
      help you take it.
    </p>

    <p class="line-em">We are a family. And we welcome you into it.</p>

    <p class="signoff">
      — Rob Gaudet, Founder<br />
      <span>Lake Charles born · Cajun Navy Ground Force</span>
    </p>

    {/*
      The closing gallery is INTENTIONALLY not built yet. It was specified as
      "remaining photos" with alt text written from what is visible in each —
      neither the count nor the contents can be known from here, and inventing
      warm descriptions of a stranger's family photographs would be making
      things up about real people. Drop the files in and this becomes a lazy-
      loaded grid in one pass.
    */}
    <section class="gallery-pending" aria-label="Family gallery, coming">
      <h2>The family, through the years.</h2>
      <p>
        The remaining photographs go here, once they're in the repository.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="story-cta">
      <p>
        If you still have someone to call, we'd be glad to help you call them.
      </p>
      <a class="btn" href="/enroll">Start their daily calls →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Narrow measure and a lot of air. This is a letter, not a landing page. */
  .story {
    max-width: 660px;
  }

  .story-head {
    margin-bottom: 2.6rem;
  }
  .story-head h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
  }
  .kicker {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--rose-deep);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .story p {
    font-size: 1.075rem;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 0 1.6rem;
  }

  .opening {
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.6vw, 1.5rem) !important;
    line-height: 1.5 !important;
    margin-bottom: 2.4rem !important;
  }

  /* ── the quiet section ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .quiet {
    margin: 3.4rem 0;
    padding: 2.4rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .quiet .line {
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3.4vw, 1.85rem) !important;
    line-height: 1.35 !important;
    margin-bottom: 1.8rem !important;
  }
  .line-em {
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-size: 1.2rem !important;
    line-height: 1.55 !important;
    color: var(--rose-deep);
  }

  /* ── photographs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .plate {
    margin: 2.6rem 0 2.8rem;
  }
  .plate.portrait {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .photo-pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  /* Placeholder frame. Quiet and clearly unfinished — never decorated to look
     like a photograph that isn't there. */
  .photo-slot {
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px dashed #cfc0a2;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    position: relative;
    color: var(--soft-ink);
  }
  .photo-slot.tall { aspect-ratio: 4 / 5; }
  .photo-slot.wide { aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; }
  .photo-slot span {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .photo-slot::after {
    content: attr(data-slot);
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 12px;
    left: 14px;
    right: 14px;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: #a2937c;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--soft-ink);
    text-align: center;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .signoff {
    margin-top: 2.6rem !important;
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-size: 1.05rem !important;
    line-height: 1.6 !important;
  }
  .signoff span {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--soft-ink);
  }

  .gallery-pending {
    margin: 3.4rem 0 0;
    padding: 2rem;
    border: 1px dashed #cfc0a2;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--paper);
  }
  .gallery-pending h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .gallery-pending p {
    font-size: 0.9rem !important;
    color: var(--soft-ink);
    margin: 0 !important;
  }

  .story-cta {
    margin-top: 3.4rem;
    padding-top: 2.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .story-cta p {
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-size: 1.2rem !important;
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem !important;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .photo-pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .quiet { margin: 2.6rem 0; padding: 1.9rem 0; }
  }
</style>
